Make thread suspension of JIT threads work on >4K page hosts, and on
conditional loop back-edges at all.

WOW64 suspends a thread that is inside the JIT by making
InternalThreadState::InterruptFaultPage read-only and waiting for the store
the JIT emits at block entries and before backward branches to fault. Two
separate things kept that from ever happening:

1. The page was a 4K member of a 4K-aligned struct. Wine can only hand the
   kernel protections at host page granularity and takes the most permissive
   protection of the guest pages sharing a host page, so on a 16K host the
   page shared its real page with the rest of the thread state and stayed
   writable no matter what NtProtectVirtualMemory was told.

   COFF caps alignas at 8K, so the struct cannot simply be 16K-aligned.
   Instead the object is allocated 16K-aligned through its own operator new,
   the member is over-sized so that a whole 16K-aligned page always fits
   inside it, and the page address and the JIT's store offset are derived
   from that at compile time (INTERRUPT_FAULT_PAGE_OFFSET /
   INTERRUPT_FAULT_PAGE_FRAME_OFFSET, GetInterruptFaultPage()). All users of
   the page go through those. 64K hosts are not covered: the page would sit
   beyond the JIT's unsigned store offset range, and the WOW64 init logs an
   error in that case.

2. DEF_OP(CondJump) never emitted the suspend check for a backward true
   target. Only the pending fall-through branch got one, so a rotated loop
   whose only back-edge is a conditional branch (any while loop) never
   stored to the page. That is independent of the host page size and also
   affects 4K hosts. The check is now emitted before a backward conditional
   branch; it is a plain store and touches neither NZCV nor any allocated
   register.

Reproduced with suspendtest32.exe: a thread spinning in `while (!stop)
counter++` could not be suspended at all (SuspendThread blocked
indefinitely). With both parts, 20/20 suspend/resume cycles succeed with a
worst-case latency of 14 ms. smctest32.exe is unaffected.

